  "Question": "Does calculating with the complement of “at least once” here involve conditional probability?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Yes",
    "B": "No",
    "C": "We must first know that the first shot was a hit",
    "D": "Only the classical probability model does not require it"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Interpreting “here” as the independent-shot setting, complementary events and independence are sufficient. “No hits” is the complement of “at least one hit,” so\n\\[\nP(\\text{at least one hit})=1-P(\\text{no hits}).\n\\]\nIndependence lets us find the probability of no hits by multiplying the individual miss probabilities. No additional event, such as the first shot being a hit, is assumed known to have occurred, so conditional probability is not required. Conditional probability is covered in the selective compulsory curriculum. Thus, under this interpretation, the answer is B (No).",
